7.7.m:
They deliberate: "Should the fire be established or not established by one who is going to establish the fire on the morrow?"
It is certainly to be established.
For this one who has no fire is without sacrifice.
A bound he-goat should stay for that night.
For the he-goat possesses the fiery splendor of Agni.
Thereby he establishes that fire.
It should be spotted,
for that [animal] belongs to all the gods.
He who is going to establish the fire on the morrow should observe an observance during that night.
He should not eat meat;
he should not approach a woman.
For every man possesses fire.
Agni might depart from him.
For this is his womb;
for from him he is generated who takes him out.
That which is the southern [fire] is churned out only for spiritual luster;
moreover, he makes him having the same womb with the two fires.
But it is hard to guard;
they should churn it day by day.
From anywhere whatsoever he should bring it;
for this is his unconfined body;
he confines just that.
Where he sees [fire] blazing far away, thence he should bring it;
for these deities become manifest;
he confines just them.
For one desiring food, he should bring it from a roasting place;
for this is his food-eating body;
he confines just that.
Whatever brahmin or vaiśya should be flourishing, as if an Asura, from his house he should bring it;
whatever that prosperity is, whatever the food is, he secures that.
However, he should not eat in his house after that.
Thrice he raises [the fire];
three are these worlds;
he attains just these worlds.
In that he raises [the fire],
he attains this world thereby.
In that he runs east,
[he attains] the atmosphere thereby.
In that he establishes [the fire],
[he attains] yonder [world] thereby.
Having established [it], he should not lift it up again;
that is a departure;
he becomes worse off.
Prajāpati indeed created creatures from the breaths;
they were generated.
He should lift it up by that measure,
for generation.
He should not lift it up too high;
Agni would burn up his breaths; he would die.
He should not hold it down below;
that is a departure;
he becomes worse off.

8.7.m:
A cake on eight potsherds for Agni;
a caru for Soma;
a cake on twelve potsherds for Savitṛ;
a caru for Sarasvatī;
a caru for Pūṣan;
a cake on seven potsherds for the Maruts;
a curd-mixture for the All-Gods;
a cake on one potsherd for Heaven and Earth.
A cake on eight potsherds for Agni;
a caru for Soma;
a cake on eight potsherds for Savitṛ;
a caru for Sarasvatī;
a caru for Pūṣan;
a cake on twelve potsherds for Indra and Agni;
a curd-mixture for the Maruts;
a curd-mixture for Varuṇa;
a cake on one potsherd for Ka.
"Homage to you, O spreader!
Go forth, unhampered, along the stream of ghee, together with offspring, together with abundance of wealth!"
We invoke the Maruts, the devourers, the conveyers of the sacrifice,
who take pleasure in the barley-groats.
May Indra not [forsake] us now in the battles, O gods; may we, O impetuous one, be your expiation!
The great song of him who offers oblations praises the Maruts, the expiation of the generous god.
Whatever sin we have committed in the village, in the wilderness, in the assembly, in our senses,
whatever sin we have committed, whatever offense we have committed,
whatever against the order of even a single one, that we expiate by sacrifice. Svāhā!
The workers have done the work with speech that brings comfort;
having done the work for the gods, go to your home, O bounteous ones!

8.8.m:
For Agni Anīkavat, a cake on eight potsherds in the morning;
for the Maruts Sāṃtapanas, a caru at midday;
for the Maruts Gṛhamedhins, rice-mash in the milk of all [cows] in the evening.
"O full ladle, fly away; well-filled, fly back again!
As for a price, O you of a hundred rites, let us trade food and vigor!
Give to me, I give to you; bestow on me, I bestow on you!
Bring me a gift, I bring you a gift!"
For the Maruts Krīḍins, a cake on seven potsherds in the morning.
A cake on eight potsherds for Agni;
a caru for Soma;
a cake on eight potsherds for Savitṛ;
a caru for Sarasvatī;
a caru for Pūṣan;
a cake on eleven potsherds for Indra and Agni;
a caru for Indra;
a cake on one potsherd for Viśvakarman.
"O Agni, desire the Hotṛ's office, desire the messenger's duty!
Stand upright at the sacrifice!
May Heaven and Earth protect you;
protect Heaven and Earth!
Be the maker of good sacrifice to Indra and the gods!
Enjoying this oblation of ghee, partake of it! Svāhā!"

8.9.m:
Ghee for Soma accompanied by the Fathers;
a cake on six potsherds for the Fathers seated on the sacred grass;
parched grains for the Fathers consumed by fire;
a stirred drink for Agni bearer of the funeral offering.
This for you, father, and for those who follow after you;
this for you, grandfather, and for those who follow after you;
this for you, great-grandfather, and for those who follow after you.
Here, O Fathers, exhilarate yourselves!
May we, O generous Indra, delight in seeing you well;
now praised, having your chariot seat full, go forth according to your desires; harness now your two bay steeds, O Indra!
Go away, O Fathers fond of Soma, along the deep, ancient paths;
having given to us here good wealth, bestow upon us riches consisting of all heroes!
The Fathers have exhilarated themselves.
Producing deeds through this station, for he is the heat, a broad path for the chosen;
facing west, he has reached the support, the peak of sweetness, when his own body propelled the body.
They have eaten, they have exhilarated themselves, and they have shaken off the dear ones;
the inspired poets, having their own light, have praised with a newest thought; harness now your two bay steeds, O Indra!
Homage to your fury, O Fathers!
Homage to your heat, O Fathers!
Homage to your living essence, O Fathers!
Homage to your sap, O Fathers!
Homage to your strength, O Fathers!
Homage to that which is fierce in you, O Fathers!
Homage, homage to you, O Fathers!
Svadhā to you, O Fathers!
What svadhā is here, O Fathers, where you are, that is among you;
with that, exhilarate yourselves according to your shares!
What invigorating food is here, O Fathers, where we are, that is among us;
with that may we live long!
We now call back the mind with the praise-song of Narāśaṃsa
and with the thoughts of the Fathers.
May our mind come back to us for purpose, for skill, for life,
and long to see the sun!
May the Fathers, the divine people, give us back our mind;
may we join the living throng!

8.10.m:
Whatever harm we have done to the atmosphere, the earth, or the sky, or to father or mother,
may Agni Gārhapatya release us from that sin, from whatever misdeeds we have done!
O Rudra, I make the mole your sacrificial victim;
this is your share, O Rudra;
enjoy it together with your sister Ambikā, svāhā!
O mother, we have propitiated Rudra, we have propitiated the god Tryambaka,
so that he will make us better off, so that he will make us more prosperous, so that he will settle us.
Healing for cow, for horse, for person, auspiciousness for ram and ewe;
moreover, so that healing, excellent healing may be for us!
We worship Tryambaka, fragrant increaser of prosperity;
like a cucumber from its stalk, may I be released from death, not from immortality!
You are share;
may I obtain your share!
This is your share, O Rudra;
with that provision cross beyond the Mūjavants, wearing an animal skin, with bow unstrung, Pināka in hand!
You are fuel; may we prosper!
You are the kindling stick; may we flourish!
You are splendor; place splendor in me!

8.11.m:
Intention is the ladle;
thought is the ghee;
speech is the altar;
reflection is the sacred grass;
insight is the fire;
the understood is the Agnīdh;
Vācaspati is the Hotṛ;
mind is the Upavaktṛ;
breath is the oblation;
the Sāman is the Adhvaryu;
go to Indra, svāhā!
Earth is the Hotṛ;
Sky is the Adhvaryu;
Tvaṣṭṛ is the Agnīdh;
Mitra is the Upavaktṛ.
O Vācaspati, you have sacrificed with speech's most collected vigor;
you have brought precious things to the lord of the sacrifice.
May Vācaspati drink the soma;
he has brought forth Indra for virile power, svāhā!
Soma is the precursor of Soma;
Śukra is the precursor of Śukra;
cooked for you, O Indra, are the soma-drops that cause wind, hearing invocations, svāhā!
Agni is the Hotṛ;
the two Aśvins are the two Adhvaryus;
Rudra is the Agnīdh;
Bṛhaspati is the Upavaktṛ.
O Vācaspati, heart-piercing by name, Vācaspati has drunk the soma;
he has placed heroic vigor in us, svāhā!
Soma is the precursor of Soma;
Śukra is the precursor of Śukra;
cooked for you, O Indra, are the soma-drops that cause wind, hearing invocations, svāhā!

8.12.m:
At the impulse of the god Savitṛ, with the arms of the Aśvins, with the hands of Pūṣan, I accept you.
May Varuṇa lead you, O divine dakṣiṇā!
A horse for Yama;
by that may I attain immortality!
May there be vigor for the giver, joy for me, the receiver!
At the impulse of the god…
May Varuṇa lead you, O divine dakṣiṇā!
Gold for Agni;
a cow for Rudra;
the wives of the gods cut you, the active ones stretched you out, the protecting ones wove you;
may Varuṇa lead you, O divine dakṣiṇā!
Clothing for Bṛhaspati;
by that may I attain immortality!
May there be vigor for the giver, joy for me, the receiver!
Who gave this to whom?
Desire gave to Desire;
Desire is the giver, Desire is the receiver;
Desire entered the ocean;
with Desire I accept you;
O Desire, this is yours!
Mahāhavis is the Hotṛ;
Satyahavis is the Adhvaryu;
Acittapājas is the Agnīdh;
Acittamanas is the Upavaktṛ;
the Invincible and the Irresistible are the two Abhigaras;
Ayāsya is the Udgātṛ.
O Vācaspati, honored by name, may we honor your name;
may you honor our name;
may the cord of the gods not be cut, nor that of men!

25.8.m:
The fire that was born from the heat of Agni, from the flame of earth or from heaven,
by which Viśvakarman brought forth creatures: let his wrath avoid you, O Agni.
Do not harm this two-footed one among animals, O thousand-eyed one, being piled in the sacrifice;
accept the mayu beast, the sacrificial animal, O Agni; piling yourself with it, sit down.
May your hunger [reach] the mayu;
may your heat strike him whom we hate!
Do not harm this single-hoofed one among animals, the neighing courser among coursers;
I assign the wild gaur to you; piling yourself with it, sit down.
May your hunger [reach] the gaur;
may your heat strike him whom we hate!
This thousandfold, hundred-streamed well, opening in the midst of the surging flood,
Aditi yielding ghee for the people: do not harm her, O Agni, in the highest firmament.
I assign the wild gavaya to you; piling yourself with it, sit down.
May your hunger [reach] the gavaya;
may your heat strike him whom we hate!
This woolly one, the navel of Varuṇa, the skin of two-footed and four-footed animals,
the first birth-giver of Tvaṣṭṛ's creatures: do not harm him, O Agni, in the highest firmament.
I assign the wild ram to you; piling yourself with it, sit down.
May your hunger [reach] the ram;
may your heat strike him whom we hate!
For the goat was born from the flame of Agni; he saw the progenitor in the beginning;
through him the gods reached the summit of divinity; through him, being worthy of sacrifice, they reached the ascent.
I assign the wild śarabha to you; piling yourself with it, sit down.
May your hunger [reach] the śarabha;
may your heat strike him whom we hate!

25.9.m:
